<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Refreshes the blocklist on this machine, twice a day.

  It has to be local. ADR-0002 keeps the signing key off every server, so the
  feed is signed where the key is and published from there — moving the key into
  CI is precisely the trade that decision refused. That makes the schedule a
  human step by construction, and this file is what reduces it to one command:

      pnpm feed:agent      # copy the bootstrap, write this plist, load it

  The same script takes a status flag (loaded? last run? last exit?) and an
  uninstall flag, optionally with purge — see docs/runbooks/feed-signing.md.

  The double-brace words below are placeholders on purpose: an absolute path
  committed to a repository is right on exactly one machine, and wrong silently
  everywhere else. The installer fills them in and writes the copy launchd reads,
  and refuses a template that lost one.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>app.okolos.feed</string>

  <!--
    A bootstrap outside the agent's checkout, never a `cd` into it (B-159). The
    plist used to run `cd <checkout> && exec node tools/feed-job.mjs`; from
    2026-10-09 to 2026-10-26 the checkout was gone, every run died in the shell,
    nothing wrote the status record, and production served a seventeen-day-old
    feed while the failure count read 0 (B-149). The installer copies
    tools/feed/bootstrap.mjs and the modules it imports to ~/.okolos/bin/feed-agent;
    it recreates a missing or broken checkout at origin/main, runs the job from it,
    and writes the status record for every failure that reaches it: stage
    checkout (no checkout, or its repair outlived the run's 45-minute deadline),
    stage job (the job died without its own record), the job's own stages.

    The shell line around it covers what fails before any of that: node not found,
    the installed copy gone, or node exiting non-zero (not 75, a run refused by the
    lock) without a record carrying this launch's id (OKOLOS_LAUNCH_ID). It writes
    feed-status.json itself with /bin/sh and coreutils only (outcome failed, stage
    launch, exit, reason, time, installedOffMain when the variable below is set;
    consecutiveFailures counted on; the lastPublished
    value copied alone, never a field written after it, so the record holds one
    runId, its own; a temp file, then mv), and one line to feed.log and to this
    file's log below.
    Not recorded anywhere: launchd not starting this at all, or /bin/sh failing;
    the installer's status flag and `launchctl print` show those. No alarm reads the
    record yet: that is OBS-32 in project-observatory-dashboard, which does not read
    this file yet. The line counts a record as this launch's only when it carries
    its id; the bootstrap stamps the id into a record it adopts from a job that
    writes none, so one failure is counted once.

    Production publishes still run only from origin/main: the job fast-forwards the
    checkout itself (tools/feed-job.mjs, F2). Through a login shell because `node`
    and `pnpm` arrive from a version manager on most machines and launchd's own
    environment has almost nothing in it; measured 2026-10-03 at 0.02 s. When that
    shell finds no `node`, the one that ran the installer is used, by the PATH entry
    it was found under (/opt/homebrew/bin/node), not its versioned realpath that the
    next upgrade deletes.

    Agent mode is if-stale plus follow: a run whose served feed is younger than
    its interval records "skipped" and ends, so a login is not a publish.
  -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/sh</string>
    <string>-lc</string>
    <string>st={{STATE}}; lg={{LOGS}}; b={{BOOTSTRAP}}; s="$st/feed-status.json"
id="launch-$$-$(date +%s)"
if command -v node &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1; then n=node; else n={{NODE}}; fi
if [ ! -f "$b" ]; then c=1; why="the installed bootstrap $b is missing; reinstall with pnpm feed:agent"
elif [ "$n" != node ] &amp;&amp; [ ! -x "$n" ]; then c=127; why="no node: none on the login PATH, and $n is gone"
else OKOLOS_LAUNCH_ID="$id" "$n" "$b"; c=$?; why="node exited $c before the bootstrap wrote a record"; fi
if [ "$c" -ne 0 ] &amp;&amp; [ "$c" -ne 75 ] &amp;&amp; ! grep -q "\"runId\": \"$id\"" "$s" 2&gt;/dev/null; then
  at=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
  p=$(sed -n 's/^  "consecutiveFailures": \([0-9][0-9]*\),$/\1/p' "$s" 2&gt;/dev/null | head -n 1)
  l=$(sed -n '/^  "lastPublished": {$/,/^  }/p; /^  "lastPublished": [^{]/p' "$s" 2&gt;/dev/null | sed '$s/,$//')
  [ -n "$l" ] || l='  "lastPublished": null'
  r=$(printf '%s' "$why" | sed 's/\\/\\\\/g; s/"/\\"/g')
  o=''; [ -z "$OKOLOS_INSTALLED_OFF_MAIN" ] || o=$(printf '\n  "installedOffMain": "%s",' "$(printf '%s' "$OKOLOS_INSTALLED_OFF_MAIN" | sed 's/\\/\\\\/g; s/"/\\"/g')")
  mkdir -p "$st" "$lg"; t="$s.$$.tmp"
  printf '{\n  "startedAt": "%s",\n  "at": "%s",\n  "outcome": "failed",\n  "stage": "launch",\n  "exit": %s,\n  "reason": "%s",\n  "runId": "%s",%s\n  "recoveredCheckout": false,\n  "consecutiveFailures": %s,\n%s\n}\n' "$at" "$at" "$c" "$r" "$id" "$o" "$((${p:-0} + 1))" "$l" &gt; "$t" &amp;&amp; chmod 600 "$t" &amp;&amp; mv -f "$t" "$s"
  printf '%s failed in launch: %s\n' "$at" "$why" &gt;&gt; "$lg/feed.log"; chmod 600 "$lg/feed.log"
  echo "feed-agent: failed in launch: $why" &gt;&amp;2
fi
exit "$c"</string>
  </array>

  <!--
    The first two are the folders the launch line above writes to, the same values:
    the bootstrap and the job read them too, so an install made with OKOLOS_STATE_DIR
    or OKOLOS_LOG_DIR set records each failure once, in one file, and never falls back
    to the home folder launchd gives them.

    The secret runner is not on the command line: the job puts it in front of the
    publish step alone, so the Cloudflare token never reaches the ingest, git or
    pnpm. It is a JSON argv (paths only, no secret), empty when this machine has no
    vault. Wrangler's telemetry is off for everything the job starts.

    The next three tell the bootstrap where the checkout is and what to recreate
    it from: a worktree of the clone the installer ran in, or, when that clone is
    gone too, a fresh clone of its origin (empty when it has none). The last is
    empty unless the agent was installed off origin/main with the installer's
    allow-branch flag; then it names the branch and commits, and every record of
    a run carries it as installedOffMain: the launch line's, the bootstrap's and
    the job's, a stop by signal and a record adopted from an older job included.
  -->
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>OKOLOS_STATE_DIR</key>
    <string>{{STATE_DIR}}</string>
    <key>OKOLOS_LOG_DIR</key>
    <string>{{LOG_DIR}}</string>
    <key>OKOLOS_AGENT_CHECKOUT</key>
    <string>{{CHECKOUT}}</string>
    <key>OKOLOS_SOURCE_REPO</key>
    <string>{{SOURCE_REPO}}</string>
    <key>OKOLOS_ORIGIN_URL</key>
    <string>{{ORIGIN}}</string>
    <key>OKOLOS_INSTALLED_OFF_MAIN</key>
    <string>{{OFF_MAIN}}</string>
    <key>OKOLOS_SECRET_RUNNER</key>
    <string>{{RUNNER}}</string>
    <key>WRANGLER_SEND_METRICS</key>
    <string>false</string>
  </dict>

  <!--
    43200 seconds is twelve hours, which is the source's own cycle as recorded in
    ADR-0010. launchd runs a missed StartInterval as soon as it can after a wake,
    which is the behaviour this wants: a laptop that was closed overnight
    refreshes on opening rather than waiting for the next slot. A run is bounded
    by one deadline, 45 minutes from the bootstrap's start with the checkout repair
    included (the job's own thirty-minute watchdog sits inside it), so launchd
    never skips a slot behind a hung one.
  -->
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>43200</integer>

  <!--
    And once at load, so installing the agent refreshes the feed instead of
    promising to in twelve hours. At every later login the same run finds the
    served feed fresh and ends.
  -->
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Not `KeepAlive`: this is a task that finishes, and keeping it alive would
    restart it in a loop. The job writes its own rotated, owner-only log and a
    status record next to its state. launchd's streams catch the launch line's
    one line when node or the bootstrap cannot start, one summary line per failed
    run, and the job's own stderr, which the bootstrap passes through as it
    arrives (a crash's error and stack; feed.log gets them too). The bootstrap and
    the job cap that file at start.
  -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>{{LOG_DIR}}/feed.launchd.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>{{LOG_DIR}}/feed.launchd.log</string>

  <!--
    Nice, because a blocklist refresh is never the most important thing the
    machine is doing.
  -->
  <key>Nice</key>
  <integer>5</integer>

  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
